# =============================================================================
# CADDY CONFIGURATION - PCS Template
# =============================================================================
# Container routing is handled via Docker labels (caddy-docker-proxy)
# This file defines global config and reusable snippets only
# =============================================================================

{
    log {
        output stdout
        format console
    }

    # Let's Encrypt email for certificate registration (used for sslip.io domains)
    email {$PCS_EMAIL}

    # Pin ACME to Let's Encrypt only. ZeroSSL was the default fallback and was
    # failing every attempt with "Invalid signature on JWS request", doubling
    # the failed-obtain rate for no benefit. With one healthy issuer we still
    # tolerate LE outages via the internal-CA fallback below.
    acme_ca https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory

    # Default issuer chain for any site without an explicit `tls` directive —
    # in practice that's all *.sslip.io labels (including ones emitted by
    # third-party app compose files we don't control). Caddy tries LE first;
    # when it fails (rate limits, malformed hostnames from empty
    # ${PUBLIC_IP_DASH} interpolation, etc.) it falls through to the internal
    # CA and issues a self-signed cert. Browsers warn on self-signed, but
    # nobody is hitting the malformed hostnames anyway — the point is to
    # break the every-5-minutes ACME retry storm that pegs Caddy's CPU.
    #
    # 168h leaf lifetime → Caddy renews each host every ~4-5 days instead of
    # retrying ACME every 5 minutes per host. Bounds CPU even at 100+
    # services with broken labels: ~22 cert renewals/day total, vs. the
    # previous ~28 800/day storm. Stays inside the 7-day default lifetime of
    # the internal intermediate CA, so we don't need `sign_with_root`.
    cert_issuer acme
    cert_issuer internal {
        lifetime 168h
    }
}

# Custom CA TLS for gateway-routed and nip.io domains
# Gateway trusts this CA, so no LE needed for these routes
# nip.io domains also use this custom CA for consistency
(gateway_tls) {
    # Restore original host when request comes through CF worker
    # The CF worker rewrites Host to nip.io for routing, but preserves
    # the original nsl.sh domain in X-Original-Host. Restoring it ensures
    # apps generate correct redirect URLs and cookie domains.
    @has_original_host header X-Original-Host *
    request_header @has_original_host Host {http.request.header.X-Original-Host}
    tls /certs/cert.pem /certs/key.pem
}

# No :80 block needed - caddy-docker-proxy handles HTTP→HTTPS redirects
# via container labels, and Caddy's internal ACME handler responds to
# /.well-known/acme-challenge/* requests automatically
#
# Certificate strategy:
#   - Gateway-routed domains (e.g., admin-${DOMAIN}): custom CA (gateway_tls)
#   - nip.io domains (e.g., admin-${PUBLIC_IP_DASH}.nip.io): custom CA (gateway_tls)
#   - sslip.io domains (e.g., admin-${PUBLIC_IP_DASH}.sslip.io): Let's Encrypt,
#     with internal-CA fallback (see global cert_issuer chain above)
#
# Most routes are via container labels in docker-compose.yml
# Root domains below use configurable DEFAULT_SERVICE_HOST/PORT (defaults to maison:80)

# =============================================================================
# ROOT DOMAIN ROUTING - Configurable default service
# =============================================================================
# Configure via .pcs.env:
#   DEFAULT_SERVICE_HOST=maison   (container name or host.docker.internal for host ports)
#   DEFAULT_SERVICE_PORT=80       (port the service listens on)
#
# `maison` is the AppShield gate in the maison stack — the authenticated front
# door to the dashboard. Pointing this at `maison-app` would publish the
# dashboard with no auth at all. Default was casaos:8080 before CasaOS was removed.
# =============================================================================

# Root domain via gateway (custom CA)
{$DOMAIN} {
    import gateway_tls
    reverse_proxy {$DEFAULT_SERVICE_HOST}:{$DEFAULT_SERVICE_PORT}
}

# Root domain via nip.io (custom CA)
{$PUBLIC_IP_DASH}.nip.io {
    import gateway_tls
    reverse_proxy {$DEFAULT_SERVICE_HOST}:{$DEFAULT_SERVICE_PORT}
}

# Root domain via sslip.io (Let's Encrypt)
{$PUBLIC_IP_DASH}.sslip.io {
    reverse_proxy {$DEFAULT_SERVICE_HOST}:{$DEFAULT_SERVICE_PORT}
}

# =============================================================================
# DEX SITE - auth-${DOMAIN} (+ nip.io / sslip.io variants)
# =============================================================================
# Imported by the `dex` service's caddy_* labels in docker-compose.yml. The
# labels there declare only the hostnames; everything below (TLS choice,
# connector memory, upstream) lives here because the connector-memory rules
# need directives — named matchers, `uri`, `handle_response` — that do not fit
# into flat caddy-docker-proxy labels.
#
# WHY CONNECTOR MEMORY EXISTS
# Dex is a pure broker: it holds no session of its own and re-runs a connector
# on every authorization request. Its connector chooser is therefore shown on
# EVERY app login, even though the upstream IdP (Authelia, or the Yundera IdP)
# still has a live session and answers instantly. The user experience is: log
# in to app A with a password, then on app B, C, D... click "Log in with X"
# once each, with no credentials ever asked for again. That click carries no
# information Dex did not already have five seconds earlier — it is pure
# friction, and it is the only thing standing between this PCS and real SSO.
#
# Dex auto-selects a connector only when EXACTLY ONE is configured
# (`oauth2.alwaysShowLoginScreen` exists to force the page back on in that
# case). This PCS normally has two — `authelia` (Local Account) and `yundera`
# (Yundera Login) — plus any drop-ins, so the chooser is unavoidable upstream.
#
# So we remember the choice at the edge instead. Dex accepts a `connector_id`
# query parameter on /auth and skips straight to that connector — a supported,
# documented parameter, not a hack. The three rules below record which door the
# user walked through and replay it on the next authorization request.
# =============================================================================
(dex_router) {
    # --- 1. REMEMBER ---------------------------------------------------------
    # The chooser's buttons link to /auth/<connector-id>?<original query> (Dex
    # copies the query verbatim). A hit on that path is the user picking a door,
    # so stamp the choice into a cookie. Host-only (no Domain=), so each of the
    # three auth hostnames keeps its own memory. 2592000s = 30 days, matching
    # the PCS-wide session policy (Authelia session, AppShield
    # SESSION_DURATION_HOURS). SameSite=Lax and not Strict: the request arriving
    # here is a top-level navigation from a DIFFERENT host (the app), which
    # Strict would strip the cookie from — defeating the whole feature.
    @dex_pick path_regexp dexpick ^/auth/([^/]+)$
    header @dex_pick +Set-Cookie "dex_connector={re.dexpick.1}; Path=/; Max-Age=2592000; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"

    # --- 2. FORGET -----------------------------------------------------------
    # Escape hatch. Without it the first choice is sticky for 30 days and a user
    # who wants their other identity (Local Account vs Yundera Login) has no way
    # back to the chooser short of clearing cookies. Append `&switch=1` to any
    # app's login URL, or hit https://auth-<domain>/auth?switch=1 directly.
    @dex_switch {
        path /auth /auth/
        query switch=1
    }
    header @dex_switch +Set-Cookie "dex_connector=; Path=/; Max-Age=0; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"

    # --- 3. REPLAY -----------------------------------------------------------
    # A fresh authorization request (client_id present) with a remembered
    # connector and no explicit choice gets connector_id injected. This is an
    # INTERNAL rewrite, not a redirect: the browser's URL is untouched, which
    # keeps {http.request.orig_uri} pointing at the un-replayed request for the
    # self-heal in rule 4.
    #   - `not query connector_id=*` — an app that asks for a specific connector
    #     wins over the cookie, and it also makes a redirect loop impossible.
    #   - `not query switch=1` — rule 2 is clearing the cookie on this very
    #     request; replaying it would defeat the escape hatch.
    #   - the capture is [A-Za-z0-9_-] so a junk cookie cannot inject query
    #     syntax into the rewritten URI.
    @dex_replay {
        path /auth /auth/
        query client_id=*
        not query connector_id=*
        not query switch=1
        header_regexp dexconn Cookie dex_connector=([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)
    }
    uri @dex_replay query +connector_id {re.dexconn.1}

    # --- 4. SELF-HEAL --------------------------------------------------------
    # Connectors come and go: ensure-yundera-login.sh REMOVES the `yundera`
    # connector when the PCS loses its provider credential, and ensure-dex.sh
    # omits `authelia` while the local account is unclaimed. Dex answers an
    # unknown connector_id with a 400 error page, so a stale cookie would lock
    # the user out of every app for the full 30 days — and it would do it at
    # exactly the moment the fallback connector matters most.
    #
    # This proxy therefore handles only requests that were replayed AND carry
    # our cookie; on a 400 it clears the memory and bounces the browser back to
    # the original, un-replayed URL. That retry has no cookie, so rule 3 does
    # not fire, the request falls through to the plain proxy below, and the
    # chooser renders. Termination is structural: the cookie can only be
    # cleared, never re-set, along this path.
    @dex_replayed {
        path /auth /auth/
        query connector_id=*
        header_regexp Cookie dex_connector=[A-Za-z0-9_-]
    }
    reverse_proxy @dex_replayed dex:5556 {
        @dex_connector_gone status 400
        handle_response @dex_connector_gone {
            header +Set-Cookie "dex_connector=; Path=/; Max-Age=0; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"
            redir {http.request.orig_uri} 302
        }
    }

    # Everything else: /token, /keys, /userinfo, /callback, the chooser itself,
    # and any /auth whose 400 is genuinely the app's fault.
    #
    # `dex:5556` rather than caddy-docker-proxy's `{{upstreams 5556}}`: that
    # template is only expanded in a LABEL, and this block is in the base
    # Caddyfile. The container name resolves on the `pcs` network, which both
    # dex and mesh-router-caddy join.
    reverse_proxy dex:5556
}

# Same, plus the custom-CA TLS used by the gateway-routed and nip.io hostnames.
(dex_router_tls) {
    import gateway_tls
    import dex_router
}

# =============================================================================
# CUSTOM DOMAIN CATCH-ALL
# =============================================================================
# NOTE: This block is dropped by caddy-docker-proxy during config regeneration.
# The mesh-router-caddy entrypoint injects equivalent config via Admin API:
#   1. TLS automation policy with on_demand + internal issuer
#   2. Catch-all HTTP route (no host matcher, appended last)
#
# This Caddyfile block is kept for documentation purposes only.
# The actual config is injected by entrypoint.sh after each reload.
#
# What it does:
#   - Handles any domain pointed at this server that doesn't match other routes
#   - Auto-generates self-signed certs (issuer internal) for unknown SNI values
#   - Works with Cloudflare "Full" mode (non-strict) - CF doesn't verify cert CA
# =============================================================================
#
#:443 {
#    tls {
#        on_demand
#        issuer internal
#    }
#    reverse_proxy {$DEFAULT_SERVICE_HOST}:{$DEFAULT_SERVICE_PORT}
#}
